/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Median of Two Sorted Arrays
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Arrays
Difficulty : Hard

Problem:
Given two sorted arrays, return the median of the combined
sorted array.

Note:
This solution uses the Merge Approach with O(m + n) time
complexity. Although accepted, the optimal solution uses
Binary Search and achieves O(log(min(m, n))) time complexity.

Approach:
Since both arrays are already sorted, we merge them into a
single sorted array using the merge process from Merge Sort.

Once the merged array is obtained, finding the median becomes
straightforward. If the total number of elements is odd, the
middle element is the median. Otherwise, the median is the
average of the two middle elements.

Why does this work?

Merging two sorted arrays preserves the sorted order of all
elements. Therefore, after merging, the median can be found
directly using its definition without any additional sorting.

Algorithm:
1. Create an empty array to store the merged elements.
2. Compare the current elements of both arrays.
3. Insert the smaller element into the merged array.
4. Continue until one array is exhausted.
5. Append the remaining elements of the other array.
6. Compute the median based on the total number of elements.

Time Complexity : O(m + n)

Reason:
Each element from both arrays is visited exactly once during
the merge process.

Space Complexity : O(m + n)

Reason:
An additional array is used to store all merged elements.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {

        // Stores the merged sorted array.
        vector<int> merged;

        int first = 0;
        int second = 0;

        // Merge both sorted arrays.
        while (first < nums1.size() && second < nums2.size()) {

            if (nums1[first] < nums2[second]) {
                merged.push_back(nums1[first]);
                first++;
            }
            else {
                merged.push_back(nums2[second]);
                second++;
            }
        }

        // Append remaining elements from the first array.
        while (first < nums1.size()) {
            merged.push_back(nums1[first]);
            first++;
        }

        // Append remaining elements from the second array.
        while (second < nums2.size()) {
            merged.push_back(nums2[second]);
            second++;
        }

        int totalSize = merged.size();

        // Even number of elements.
        if (totalSize % 2 == 0) {
            return (merged[totalSize / 2 - 1] + merged[totalSize / 2]) / 2.0;
        }

        // Odd number of elements.
        return merged[totalSize / 2];
    }
};
